WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:05.040
No one is using Bumble.
This app was at the top of their game.&nbsp;

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:11.680
58 million users. A $2.2 billion&nbsp;
IPO. Over a billion in revenue.&nbsp;

00:00:11.680 --> 00:00:16.320
Now, look at that stock graph.
Down 95%.&nbsp;

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:21.280
Revenue is falling, profit has&nbsp;
collapsed, and users are fleeing?&nbsp;

00:00:21.280 --> 00:00:23.840
What happened?
As it turns out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:28.640
something much bigger is happening.
Dating apps across the board are dying,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:37.920
and Bumble is the biggest loser.

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:42.000
To understand what happened, we need&nbsp;
to look at why Bumble worked. And that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:47.440
brings us to its founder: Whitney Wolfe&nbsp;
Herd, who actually co-founded Tinder.&nbsp;

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:52.240
Reportedly, Herd gave Tinder its name and&nbsp;
logo, leading its aggressive marketing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:57.520
on campus. Pretty pivotal.
Then, she was forced out.

00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:02.880
A lawsuit quickly emerged, which eventually&nbsp;
led to a settlement of a bit over $1 million.&nbsp;

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:10.400
Sounds like a lot, but considering how big&nbsp;
Tinder was becoming, Herd was done pretty dirty.&nbsp;

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:15.040
But, she soon had another opportunity,&nbsp;
from Tinder’s rival: Badoo.

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:20.000
Andrey Andreev, the entrepreneur behind&nbsp;
the dating app with over 425 million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:24.800
registered users worldwide, had spent years&nbsp;
trying to break into the American market.&nbsp;

00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:28.080
With mixed results…
Then he saw the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:34.800
lawsuit. Herd seemed like the perfect fit.
He wanted her to become Badoo’s CMO. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:39.920
she pitched something else: Bumble.
This time, if two people matched,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:44.160
the woman had to message first.
If you didn’t use these apps,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:48.458
that might sound strange. Why do that?
To quote Herd herself:&nbsp;

00:01:48.458 --> 00:01:52.800
[Whitney Wolfe Herd]: "What if women make the&nbsp;
first move, send the first message? And if they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:58.720
don’t, the match disappears after 24 hours, like&nbsp;
in Cinderella, the pumpkin and the carriage? What&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:04.720
if we could hardwire that into a product?”
Andreev didn’t love the idea… but,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:07.840
he trusted Herd.
He offered $10 million in funding,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:14.320
but in exchange, he would own 79% of Bumble.
Though, Herd would have total creative control,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:18.640
and Bumble could use Badoo’s software&nbsp;
infrastructure, a huge head start.

00:02:18.640 --> 00:02:23.040
In just the first month, Bumble&nbsp;
passed 100,000 downloads.&nbsp;

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:26.720
In eight months, over 5 million&nbsp;
unique conversations in-app.&nbsp;

00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:32.880
Then from 1 million downloads in 2015,&nbsp;
to 8 million the next year. To 12. To 17.&nbsp;

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:39.040
Revenue was climbing and passed 200 million.
Women loved this app, but so did a lot of men,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:42.320
who often felt a lot of pressure&nbsp;
to make the first move on Tinder.&nbsp;

00:02:42.320 --> 00:02:45.040
Plus, this was by design.
[Whitney Wolfe Herd]: ”I’m&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.040 --> 00:02:49.440
so tired of this notion that women only need&nbsp;
to support women, why can’t we all support&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:54.480
each other? I’ve run into women who can be&nbsp;
highly problematic, detrimental and mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:57.680
just like I’ve seen that in men.”
The one difference,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.680 --> 00:03:04.560
of women messaging first, really was huge.
Then, out of nowhere, came a road bump. A big one.

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:11.840
While Bumble was doing great, Andreev's empire was&nbsp;
imploding. But not for reasons you might expect.

00:03:11.840 --> 00:03:15.840
As the PR was getting worse and worse,&nbsp;
Andreev sold his stake in MagicLab,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:21.120
the parent of Bumble and Badoo,&nbsp;
to Blackstone for $3 billion.&nbsp;

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:24.960
Bumble was now owned by the world’s&nbsp;
largest private equity firm.&nbsp;

00:03:24.960 --> 00:03:29.440
Though, Herd kept her 19% stake,&nbsp;
and the entire operation was merged&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:34.720
into Bumble Inc., with her as CEO.
I think Blackstone saw that the real&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.720 --> 00:03:38.960
thing making money was Bumble.
So it worked out well for Herd,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.960 --> 00:03:44.800
and by now, Bumble was unstoppable.
It was America’s fastest-growing dating app.&nbsp;

00:03:44.800 --> 00:03:48.800
Revenue jumped from $240&nbsp;
million to over $330 million.

00:03:48.800 --> 00:03:55.280
Then, came the moment everything was&nbsp;
building toward: February 11, 2021. The IPO.&nbsp;

00:03:55.280 --> 00:03:59.840
It started with shares priced&nbsp;
at $43, raising $2.2 billion.&nbsp;

00:03:59.840 --> 00:04:05.520
The stock opened at $76, jumping&nbsp;
more than 76% in a single day.&nbsp;

00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:10.160
Almost overnight, the market valued&nbsp;
Bumble at roughly $14 billion.

00:04:10.160 --> 00:04:13.760
At 31, Whitney Wolfe Herd was now a billionaire,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.760 --> 00:04:18.880
and still with an 11.6% stake.
Everything was going great.&nbsp;

00:04:18.880 --> 00:04:23.280
Then Bumble made a change,&nbsp;
and a critical one at that.&nbsp;

00:04:23.280 --> 00:04:31.280
One which started a long chain of dominoes&nbsp;
that would lead to the app’s collapse.

00:04:34.480 --> 00:04:37.120
Something strange happened after the IPO.

00:04:37.120 --> 00:04:42.160
Just months later, on September 9th, 2021,&nbsp;
Bumble announced Blackstone would be selling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:45.280
18,000,000 shares at $54 a share.
About a billion dollars.&nbsp;

00:04:45.280 --> 00:04:50.400
So, why does this matter?
Well, it gives us some very important context.

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:54.720
Private equity firms like Blackstone Inc.&nbsp;
are built to buy companies, grow them fast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.720 --> 00:04:59.920
then sell at the highest possible price.
That’s on top of the $2 billion of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.920 --> 00:05:04.080
shares it sold during the IPO.
Though it wasn’t all their shares&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.080 --> 00:05:08.400
in Bumble… keep that in mind.
But investors began to think:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:13.600
Maybe Bumble had already peaked?
And, in a way, they were right.&nbsp;

00:05:13.600 --> 00:05:18.960
Nonetheless, this sent the stock down.
But Blackstone wasn't done… yet. Keep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.960 --> 00:05:22.000
them in mind for later.
In the meantime, there was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:28.560
finally a bit of good news. In 2021, Bumble&nbsp;
Inc. reported $286 million in net income.&nbsp;

00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:33.920
Its first profitable year ever.
Then in February 2023, Bumble forecasted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.920 --> 00:05:38.800
revenue growth, even above projections.
This was big, but something else made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.800 --> 00:05:43.520
this seem even better. Herd’s old&nbsp;
opponent: Tinder, was struggling.&nbsp;

00:05:43.520 --> 00:05:47.840
Tinder, aka their parent Match&nbsp;
Group, forecasted revenue below&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.840 --> 00:05:54.640
expectations. Their first ever quarterly decline.
While Bumble’s paying users increased 25% to 2.6&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.640 --> 00:06:00.640
million; 147,000 growing quarter over quarter.
Tinder was falling off! At least,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.640 --> 00:06:04.400
that’s how it seemed.
So the board approved a $150&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.400 --> 00:06:07.920
million stock buyback program.
Basically, they thought the market&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.920 --> 00:06:12.720
undervalued Bumble, and this was big.
Showing they were succeeding while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.720 --> 00:06:17.360
Tinder struggled, pushed the market cap&nbsp;
from about $3 billion to $6.5 billion.&nbsp;

00:06:17.360 --> 00:06:23.120
Not an all-time peak, but this was a great sign.
Maybe Bumble was just getting started?&nbsp;

00:06:23.120 --> 00:06:26.560
Unfortunately, you might&nbsp;
notice the rest of the graph.&nbsp;

00:06:26.560 --> 00:06:32.240
Tinder wasn’t really struggling. It was more&nbsp;
that the entire industry began to struggle.&nbsp;

00:06:32.240 --> 00:06:39.200
Global dating app downloads had fallen from 287&nbsp;
million in 2020 to around 237 million by 2023.&nbsp;

00:06:39.200 --> 00:06:44.320
A 17% drop in just three years.
Tinder's paying users started shrinking…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.320 --> 00:06:50.160
and kept shrinking, down to 9.3 million, with&nbsp;
quarterly revenue dropping to $491 million.&nbsp;

00:06:50.160 --> 00:06:54.960
Match Group, Tinder’s parent company,&nbsp;
had lost nearly 80% of its peak value.&nbsp;

00:06:54.960 --> 00:06:59.680
Then the same happened to Bumble.&nbsp;
A gigantic share drop in August.&nbsp;

00:06:59.680 --> 00:07:04.720
So, what was happening?
Among college and graduate students in the U.S.,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.720 --> 00:07:11.360
79% said they weren’t using dating apps at all.
By 2023, around 61% of users&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.360 --> 00:07:16.560
were between 30 and 49 years old.
I think many millennials, the generation that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.560 --> 00:07:23.280
helped dating apps explode, were slowly aging out&nbsp;
of app-based dating, while Gen Z weren’t as keen.

00:07:23.280 --> 00:07:28.640
In late 2023, Herd stepped down from the&nbsp;
CEO role and was replaced by Lidiane Jones,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.640 --> 00:07:32.160
who led Salesforce’s Slack.
Herd might have been a good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.160 --> 00:07:40.720
visionary leader for Bumble, Jones… wasn’t.
She had big plans, but they were all pretty awful.&nbsp;

00:07:40.720 --> 00:07:43.680
First, she laid off 350 employees.&nbsp;

00:07:43.680 --> 00:07:48.560
Then just two months later, she announced&nbsp;
a new feature called “Opening Moves.”&nbsp;

00:07:48.560 --> 00:07:52.960
For the first time in Bumble’s history,&nbsp;
women no longer had to message first.&nbsp;

00:07:52.960 --> 00:07:57.120
Instead, women could post prompts on&nbsp;
their profile for men to respond to.&nbsp;

00:07:57.120 --> 00:08:01.840
Not exactly the same as Tinder,&nbsp;
but it was pretty darn close.&nbsp;

00:08:01.840 --> 00:08:05.600
"It's basically just Tinder now"
"Giving both parties the ability to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.600 --> 00:08:11.200
drive first engagement while pretending it's women&nbsp;
is a good way to have your cake and eat it too"&nbsp;

00:08:11.200 --> 00:08:15.280
"The novelty of women initiating is&nbsp;
why I opened an account with Bumble;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.280 --> 00:08:20.320
take that away and Bumble will just fall out&nbsp;
of the circulation of apps I check in on"&nbsp;

00:08:20.320 --> 00:08:23.920
I genuinely don’t know what&nbsp;
Jones was thinking. You need to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.920 --> 00:08:30.720
change things for sure, but, why do this?
Why remove what makes your app stand out?&nbsp;

00:08:30.720 --> 00:08:40.480
Unfortunately for Bumble,&nbsp;
things were only getting worse.

00:08:40.480 --> 00:08:47.120
We just passed 900,000 subscribers, and&nbsp;
I just want to say thank you all so much.&nbsp;

00:08:47.120 --> 00:08:52.560
This ride has been awesome, and&nbsp;
we're almost at 1 million now!&nbsp;

00:08:52.560 --> 00:08:54.160
Setup
Unsurprisingly, Bumble’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.160 --> 00:09:00.720
market value had collapsed to around $1 billion.
Then, after just one year as CEO, Lidiane Jones&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.720 --> 00:09:05.760
quit, along with Bumble’s CFO and CMO.
The entire leadership team behind the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.760 --> 00:09:10.545
rebrand was suddenly gone, leaving&nbsp;
Herd back in control as CEO.&nbsp;

00:09:10.545 --> 00:09:14.080
[Whitney Wolfe Herd]:“I am deeply grateful&nbsp;
for the transformative work Lidiane has led&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.080 --> 00:09:19.200
during such a pivotal time for Bumble, and her&nbsp;
leadership has been instrumental in building a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.200 --> 00:09:23.280
strong foundation for our future,”.
I’m pretty sure Herd was just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.280 --> 00:09:27.120
saving face for Jones here.
Regardless, she was back,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.120 --> 00:09:30.960
though she didn’t really want to be.
[Whitney Wolfe Herd]: "I had no intention&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.960 --> 00:09:36.880
of coming back. Bumble needs me back. It's an&nbsp;
extension of me to some degree, and watching it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.880 --> 00:09:41.200
fall from its peak has been very hard."
She now had to clean up this mess,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.200 --> 00:09:45.040
and unfortunately, the industry&nbsp;
was in a pretty bad spot.

00:09:45.040 --> 00:09:51.360
In just 12 months, Tinder, Bumble and Hinge&nbsp;
lost roughly 1.1 million users in the UK.&nbsp;

00:09:51.360 --> 00:09:58.080
78% of Gen X, 80% of millennials and 79%&nbsp;
of Gen Z were burnt out of dating apps.&nbsp;

00:09:58.080 --> 00:10:03.120
“Over time, the unfortunate misgivings of&nbsp;
being on a dating app can cause someone to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.120 --> 00:10:06.880
lose hope in the dating process&nbsp;
and finding the right person.”

00:10:06.880 --> 00:10:09.600
But it gets worse.
By this time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.600 --> 00:10:15.280
Blackstone had also cashed out big time.
They sold 11 million shares in 2023,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.280 --> 00:10:20.400
then another 11 million in 2024, each&nbsp;
time, the price per share dropping lower.&nbsp;

00:10:20.400 --> 00:10:24.000
They had made about $2 billion from&nbsp;
the investment, though Bumble’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.000 --> 00:10:29.280
stock was now down 94%.
And, we’re still not done.&nbsp;

00:10:29.280 --> 00:10:35.040
Match Group had returned, even stronger.
Not with Tinder. But Hinge.&nbsp;

00:10:35.040 --> 00:10:39.440
By 2025, Hinge was generating&nbsp;
$691 million in revenue.&nbsp;

00:10:39.440 --> 00:10:42.800
Just seven years earlier,&nbsp;
it had made only $8 million.&nbsp;

00:10:42.800 --> 00:10:46.320
The app had exploded to around&nbsp;
32 million users worldwide,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.320 --> 00:10:50.640
with nearly 2 million paying subscribers.
By this point, a date was being set up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.640 --> 00:10:55.520
on Hinge every two seconds.
So why was Hinge exploding?&nbsp;

00:10:55.520 --> 00:10:59.680
Hinge had a unique goal:&nbsp;
Get the user to delete it.&nbsp;

00:10:59.680 --> 00:11:06.000
Sounds weird, but the goal was to get the users&nbsp;
a successful relationship, so they could move on.&nbsp;

00:11:06.000 --> 00:11:11.440
Instead of giving users unlimited swipes, the app&nbsp;
limited how many likes could be sent each day.&nbsp;

00:11:11.440 --> 00:11:16.080
And this completely changed behavior,&nbsp;
because on Tinder… and Bumble,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.080 --> 00:11:20.400
many would just swipe on every profile.
Now, you had to be more intentional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.400 --> 00:11:25.040
about who you matched with instead of&nbsp;
mindlessly swiping hundreds of profiles.&nbsp;

00:11:25.040 --> 00:11:29.040
Not only that, Hinge became&nbsp;
especially popular with Gen Z,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.040 --> 00:11:31.520
at least, those still using dating apps.

00:11:31.520 --> 00:11:35.920
Almost half of its user base&nbsp;
is between 18 and 29. Plus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.920 --> 00:11:41.040
many users are paying users.
So what did Herd do?&nbsp;

00:11:41.040 --> 00:11:45.360
Layoffs, around 240 employees were&nbsp;
cut in a single wave, bringing total&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.360 --> 00:11:51.280
layoffs to nearly 600 people in just 18 months.
Meanwhile, Bumble's user base had already fallen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.280 --> 00:11:57.200
from 58 million users to around 50 million.
Then came the bigger shift in three parts.&nbsp;

00:11:57.200 --> 00:12:02.880
First, Bumble Inc. stopped trying to attract&nbsp;
everyone and instead only the “right people”.&nbsp;

00:12:02.880 --> 00:12:07.120
Basically, Herd launched a major&nbsp;
overhaul. What she called a “Quality&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.120 --> 00:12:12.080
Reset”. Removing all kinds of fake accounts,&nbsp;
or “low-intent” users who lowered the dating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.080 --> 00:12:16.640
experience and made it more “spammy”.
Next came killing the swipe entirely.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.640 --> 00:12:21.120
And with that change, Bumble also moved&nbsp;
entirely away from the very thing that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.120 --> 00:12:25.280
made it famous: women messaging first.
[Whitney Wolfe Herd]:"we will be basically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.280 --> 00:12:29.920
bottling the essence of what was always&nbsp;
meant to be women making the first move — a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.920 --> 00:12:34.640
confident experience, a safe experience,&nbsp;
a dynamic experience — to get you offline&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.640 --> 00:12:39.600
in person on great dates and meetups.”
Jones had already somewhat removed that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:39.600 --> 00:12:45.280
dynamic by this point anyway, and this was&nbsp;
more an entire “reset” than what Jones did.&nbsp;

00:12:45.280 --> 00:12:51.120
But, how would they do so?
Take a guess: AI

00:12:51.120 --> 00:12:57.200
Now, instead of women or even men doing&nbsp;
the swiping, AI will become the matchmaker.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:57.200 --> 00:13:01.520
Specifically the in-app AI: Bee.
Oh boy…&nbsp;

00:13:01.520 --> 00:13:06.000
Though, it wouldn’t “encourage”&nbsp;
AI-generated photos or messages, thankfully.&nbsp;

00:13:06.000 --> 00:13:08.720
The AI tool will scan user&nbsp;
data to learn their values,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:08.720 --> 00:13:12.800
relationship goals, communication style,&nbsp;
and lifestyle through private chats.&nbsp;

00:13:12.800 --> 00:13:18.160
Once Bee considers two people compatible, both&nbsp;
are notified in the app with a summary explaining&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.160 --> 00:13:22.560
why they are a strong match.
But it doesn’t stop there.&nbsp;

00:13:22.560 --> 00:13:29.040
Bumble is basically moving to a “date chosen&nbsp;
for you” instead of opting in or swiping. Like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:29.040 --> 00:13:34.880
scrolling on TikTok vs clicking a YouTube video.
Not that swiping was really that great a method&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:34.880 --> 00:13:39.428
in the first place but annoying&nbsp;
to see another app move to AI.&nbsp;

00:13:39.428 --> 00:13:41.600
Grand Payoff
Can Bumble turn things around?&nbsp;

00:13:41.600 --> 00:13:47.200
Well, their Q1 2026 revenue&nbsp;
was also down as much as 14.1%.

00:13:47.200 --> 00:13:51.440
But, earnings increased 165%.
It seems the era of high&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:51.440 --> 00:13:56.640
growth is over. Now, Bumble is just&nbsp;
trying to stay alive and profitable.

00:13:56.640 --> 00:14:04.160
Another app with an even stranger story is BeReal.
This app exploded, but it quickly disappeared.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:04.160 --> 00:14:09.040
Fans gradually got more and more annoyed&nbsp;
with it, but even stranger, the founders&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:09.040 --> 00:14:18.560
seemed to have no plan on how to make money.
